held.text:
Prima facie it appears that respondent No. 1 and such other companies, which are mushrooming in the country under the corporate veil, are committing fraud by tempting middle class persons to invest their lifelong savings in the high hope of having fanciful returns, without there being any basis. Further, in agriculture productions, this type of high returns are impossibility in this country, where, otherwise, the farmers of this country would have become multi-millionaires by this time. It appears that, because of this empty, false promises, investors are duped and the company’s moneys are siphoned off either to sister companies or firms of the directors or their relatives.

As held in DDA v. Skipper Construction Co. (P.) Ltd. AIR 1996 SCW 2401 where the corporate character is employed for the purpose of committing illegality or for defrauding others, the Court would ignore the entity with regard to corporate company and its association of men and women and will do justice between the real persons. The concept of corporate entity was evolved to encourage and promote trade and commerce but not to commit illegalities or to defraud people. Absence of a statutory provision will not inhibit the Court while acting under article 142/226 from making appropriate orders for doing complete justice between the parties. In India, the Courts are not only Courts of law, but also the Courts of equity. In the aforesaid case, the Court passed an appropriate order attaching certain properties of the Directors of the company.

Applying the aforesaid ratio and taking into consideration the fact that large number of investors are being defrauded by employing the corporate veil, which may amount to a criminal offence, and also the fact that under Criminal Law property acquired by fraud, cheating, etc., can be followed and seized from wherever it is lying, appropriate directions could be issued by the Court to search and seize such property. Further, under article 226 of the Constitution, the Court can issue appropriate directions for not only enforcing any of the rights conferred by Part III but ‘for any other purpose’, and ‘any other purpose’ would include such public interest purpose.

In this view of the matter, following order was passed—

(a)The D.G.P. of State of Maharashtra was directed to instruct the State CID, Poona and other concerned police authorities to enquire into various frauds committed by the first respondent-company and its directors.

(b)Investigating Officers were directed to hold necessary enquiry to find out the whereabouts of the first respondent-company’s property, both movable and immovable, and whereabouts of its directors who were not traceable.

(c)Respondent Nos. 1 to 8 and the group companies were directed to forthwith state on oath, in an affidavit the following:

(i )The list of their movable and immovable properties and all assets and location thereof;

(ii )The list of their bank accounts and the particulars thereof, including the names and addresses of the banks and amounts therein;

(d)Respondent Nos. 2 to 8 were directed to furnish a list of group companies and indicate the amounts and assets, whether movable or immovable, transferred from respondent No. 1 to its group companies or any other companies in which respondent Nos. 2 to 8 were directors or shareholders;

(e)Respondent No. 9 was directed to write to all scheduled commercial banks, intimating the present order and not to permit respondent Nos. 1 to 8 and the group companies of respondent No. 1 to operate their respective bank accounts;

(f) Investigating Officers were further directed to find out the assets of the directors of the first respondent-company and whether any amount of the first respondent-company was siphoned off to any sister concerns or in the names of relatives of the directors; if necessary by taking assistance of Auditors;

(g )Investigating Officers were directed to prepare an inventory of the properties belonging to respondent Nos. 1 to 8 and the group companies mentioned in page 131 of the petition and to attach them. This was for the time being, to find out whether the properties belonging to the first respondent-company were siphoned off by the directors by defrauding the investors;

(h) The Directors of the first respondent-company were directed to co-operate with the aforesaid enquiry;

(i )Investigating Officers enquiring into the matter would only attach the property and not dispossess the persons in possession thereof after preparing the necessary panchanama; and

(j )Investigating Officers were directed to submit their interim report on or before 14-12-1998 under a sealed cover.

