Therefore, any suit, proceedings, action, question of law or facts that arise with respect to assets and the winding up of the company, shall be explicitly tried by the Company Courts. The Hon'ble Gujarat High Court in the case of Rajaratna Naranbhai Mills Co. Ltd. (In Liquidation): by its Official Liquidator v. New Quality Bobbin Works,18 has stated that when a High Court is conducting winding up proceedings of a company, a special jurisdiction is conferred on it by Section 446(2)19 of the Companies Act, 1956 by which the Company Court gets to entertain certain types of proceedings or withdraw certain types of cases pending in any court and transfer it to itself and to dispose off the same. The court further stated that it is necessary to put liberal construction to Section 446(2)20 to widen the scope of the High Court in dealing with all questions arising in winding up proceedings. The court has also described Section 446(2)21 as a residuary clause that should be given the widest import to ensure that the Company Court will have jurisdiction to entertain or dispose of any question, whether of law or fact which may relate to or arise in the course of winding-up of the company. The above observation has also been followed by the Hon'ble Karnataka High Court in the case of M/s Navajeevan Enterprises (Mysore) Private Ltd. v. T.N. Ramalingah&Anr.22

The Hon'ble Bombay High Court has also adopted the above-mentioned stand in the cases of Ajay Y. Mafatlal v. Mafatlal Dyes Chemicals Ltd.23 and In the matter of Transpower Engineering Ltd. (In Liquidation) Metal Tubes and Rolling Mills,24 In re . The court further emphasized the importance of having section 446 (2) of the Companies Act, 195625 in order to realize and recover the claims and subsisting debts owed to the company, which would have been difficult for the Official Liquidator as he would have to file multiple cases in different forums to recover the same.

Such a similar clause has also been incorporated in Section 60(5)(c) of the IBC26 wherein it has been stated that "any question of priorities or any question of law or facts, arising out of or in relation to the insolvency resolution or liquidation proceedings of the Corporate Debtor or Corporate Person under this Code."Therefore, the Adjudicating Authority has been conferred with residuary jurisdiction under Section 60(5)(c) of the IBC27 to adjudicate questions of law or fact arising from or in relation to the insolvency resolution proceedings to avoid any multiplicity of proceedings. In light of the observations made in the above-mentioned judgments, it is understood that once insolvency proceedings are initiated against a Corporate Debtor, the Adjudicating Authority has the power to usurp the powers of the relevant forum vide its residuary jurisdiction and adjudicate upon the same as long as the question of law or facts arises out of the insolvency of the Corporate Debtor.

